    "lawCode": "Rpld §651 sub 3, §§655 & 656, amd §§652 - 654, 657 - 659, Lab L",
    "lawSection": "Labor Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S6049\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the labor law, in relation to\neliminating the wage board; and to repeal subdivision 3 of section 651\nand sections 655 and 656 of such law relating thereto\n\nPURPOSE:\n\nThis bill would eliminate the wage board.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 - Repeals subdivision 3 of section 651 and sections 655 and\n656 of the labor law.\n\nSection 2 - Amends subdivision 2 and 6 of section 652 of the labor\nlaw, subdivision 2 as amended by chapter 38 of the laws of 1990 and\nsubdivision 6 as added by section 1 of part P of chapter 57 of the\nlaws of 2013.\n\nSection 3 - Amends section 653 of the labor law, as amended by chapter\n14 of the laws of 2000.\n\nSection 4 - Amends sections 654 of the labor law, as added by chapter\n619 of the laws of 1960.\n\nSection 5 - Amends subdivisions 1 and 2 of section 657 of the labor\nlaw, subdivision 1 as added by chapter 619 of the laws of 1960 and\nsubdivision 2 as amended by chapter 102 of the laws of 1968.\n\nSection 6 - Amends sections 658 of the labor law, as amended by\nchapter 443 of the laws of 1981.\n\nSection 7 - Amends section 659 of the labor law, as added by chapter\n619 of the laws of 1960.\n\nSection 8 - Effective date.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nA major policy decision as significant as the minimum wage should be a\npart of the legislative process, requiring approval by both Houses and\nthe Governor. The Governor directed the Commissioner of Labor to\nempanel the NYS Fast Food Wage Board. When the Board was selected, the\nGovernor made it clear that he wanted the minimum wage for fast food\nworkers raised to $15 an hour. He appointed individuals who shared\nthat view point, and who ultimately recommended the hourly increase\nfrom the current $8.75 to $15. The Labor Commission then rubber\nstamped that recommendation.\n\nDespite the fact that one member of the Board was supposed to be an\nindividual who represented the industry that would be affected, that\nprovision was ignored and no such individual was appointed to the\nBoard. Based upon this ill conceived action, the policy in New York\nState is now to prefer fast food restaurant workers over healthcare,\nhuman services workers, as well as numerous others. This is poor\npublic policy. To avoid having this scenario repeated, the Board\n\n\nshould be eliminated. This legislation will ensure that a future\ngovernor, whomever he or she may be, cannot again circumvent the\nlegislative process.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n\n",
